titre $  Nannega Key to the families of the myxomycetes
com$ adapt GF 2015

1,0$  Source///A Guide to Temperate Myxomycetes 1989/// An English translation by A. Feest and Y. Burggraaf of De Nederlandse Myxomyeeten by N.E. Nannenga-Bremekamp
1,20$Introduction

1,0$

1,0 $ Spores borne on stalks on the outside of the fructification $ Ceratiomyxomycetidae, Ceratiomyxales, Ceratiomyxaceae.
1,2 $ Spores free inside a fructification (the wall may be fugacious)  Myxogastromycetidae.
2,3 $ Spore mass yellow, ochraceous, red-purple, rust-brown, dark brown almost black or pale grey, rarely violet (Cribraria), typically without lilac shades. Lime not present (exceptionally it may be deposited on the peridium in the form of calcium oxalate [which does not dissolve in acid])
2,13 $ Spore mass black, dark brown, lilac-grey, rarely rust-brown or red-brown (e.g. Symphytocarpus, Comatricha and Stemonitis species which turn to rather dark brown), usually with a lilac tinge. Lime sometimes present
3,6 $ No capillitium present (rarely some very thin threads present). Pseudocapillitium sometimes present in the form of perforated or fringed plates, threads, or thick irregular tubes. Columella absent. Peridium persistent  Liceales
3,4 $ Either a capillitium present and/or a columella present or the peridium fugacious....
4,5 $ Sporangia always stalked, small, subglobose, often too small to be seen with the naked eye, pale or dark brown  Echinosteliales
4,10 $ Sporangia generally clearly visible with the naked eye; stalked or sessile, sometimes plasmodiocarpous, peridium usually persistent at least in the lower partc..   Trichiales
5,0 $ Sporangia colourless, white or very pale. Peridium fugacious except sometimes for a collar around the stalk $ Echinostelium.
5,0 $ Sporangia dark brown. Peridium persisting as plates on the capillitium or as a lobed cup at the base of the columella $ Clastoderma.
6,7 $ Small, usually sessile sporangia or rarely plasmodiocarps; sessile or stalked, the stalks granular (like a cylindrical hypothallus)
6,8 $ Sporangia if small always stalked; stalk translucent; otherwise larger, or aggregated into pseudo- or true aethalia
7,0 $ Sessile, dark, angular sporangia, with long beaded pseudocapillitial threads or tubules arising from the margins of the plates into which it dehisces. Usually on living Cladonia $ Listerellaceae.
7,0 $ Fructifications lacking capillitium-like outgrowths on the margin of peridial platelets (if present) $ Liceaceae.
8,0 $ Peridium covered with, often coloured, lime globules, ("dictydine granules"), normally also with a persistent net of thickened peridium $ Cribrariaceae.
8,9 $ Peridium not covered with lime globules, fructifications generally in the form of true aethalia or pseudoaethalia
9,0 $ Fructifications depressed, tessellated above, with T-shaped strands from the peridium downwards to the base of the sporangium $ Dictydiaethalium.
9,0 $ Fructifications pulvinate, not tessellated above. Pseudocapillitium of plates fraying at the margins or of perforated plates $ Enteridiaceae
10,0 $ Capillitium of slender solid threads, connected to the base of the sporangium and sometimes also to the peridium $ Dianemaceae.
10,11 $ Capillitium tubular, usually rather wide
11,12 $ Capillitium not birefringent; with or without spiral structure
11,0 $ Capillitium birefringent, tubes nearly always bearing spirals, sometimes only very vague ones $ Trichiaceae.
12,0 $ Capillitium of very slender, long, coiled tubules. Spore-mass dark brown. Spores in clusters $ Minakatellaceae.
12,0 $ Capillitium neither thread-like nor coiled; spore-mass not dark brown, if brown only rather pale brown and spores not in clusters $ Arcyriaceae.
13,14 $ Fructifications limeless (no calcium carbonate, which is usually dull and pale or white)   Stemonitales.
13,15 $ Peridium and sometimes also the capillitium containing lime. Capillitium usually consisting of hollow tubes (the latter are thread-like except when filled with lime)  Physarales.
14,0 $ Fructifications pseudoaethalia, component sporangia cylindrical, with a Peridial cup-like base. Capillitium of coiled (if of coiled slender tubules c.f. 12 Minakatellaceae) columnar strands attached to the base and the peridium $ Schenellaceae.
14,0 $ Stalk, if present, and capillitium seeming homy. Capillitium attached to the columella, if present, or to the base of the fructification and sometimes to the peridium. Peridium often fugacious $ Stemonitaceae.
15,0 $ Capillitial lime nodes completely, or in part, filled with solid or hollow lime globules. Peridium containing or covered with lime globules $ Physaraceae.
15,0 $ Capillitium typically limeless, if some lime is present this is crystalline. Peridium either covered with or containing lime globules or limeless and with the stalk, if present, and/or columella sometimes containing lime (e.g. Diderma placed by Lister in the Physaraceae and by Martin and Alexopoulos in the Didymiaceae. It is intended to combine Diachea, Diderma and Physarina in the new family "Didermaceae"), or the peridium covered with and holding lime crystals $ Didymiaceae

20,0$THE TAXONOMY OF MYXOMYCETES///The following keys employ a mixture of macroscopic and microscopic details. Those who do not possess a microscope (with an oil immersion lens), which is essential for some details, may have to send specimens to an expert for verification. Fully mature and dry fructifications will survive transport well if their substrate is carefully stuck to a piece of card secured to the base of a stout box. In the case of very mouldy, fragile wooden substrates on which, for example, Cribraria likes to grow it is a good policy to cover the specimens with a piece of flimsy paper e.g. cigarette paper. Specimens should be accompanied with full documentation including the substrate, date of collection, any further notes on immature colours (or the colour of the plasmodium if seen) and also whether you wish to have the specimen returned./// The first key given below leads to the families of myxomycetes found throughout the world. It is not meant for the determination of specimens but is included as a general survey, but is in case your specimen belongs to a genus not described in this book (not known from the Netherlands). You can then look it up under the family description where a short reference is made to untreated genera. The second key is to the genera in this book.